WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.500
Kobe Bryant's legacy lives on even in a galaxy
far, far away.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.340
On Sunday, January 26, 2020, the former Los
Angeles Lakers player, husband, and father

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.580
of four died at the age of 41 in a helicopter
crash.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.230
The aircraft which was carrying Bryant, his
13-year-old daughter Gianna, and seven other

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.939
individuals, including the pilot is said to
have been flying through inclement, foggy

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:25.099
weather conditions, which may have contributed
to the fatal wreck.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.340
Bryant left behind his wife, Vanessa, and
three other daughters.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:33.020
In the wake of Bryant's death, fans have posted
online countless tributes to him, remembering

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.480
Bryant for the person he was on and off the
court.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.980
During that remembrance, Star Wars enthusiasts
have recalled Bryant's connection to the hallowed

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.860
property: there's a character that was actually
inspired by him.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.220
"Welcome to the Kobe show.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.020
Take two."

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.780
The Kobe Bryant-inspired Star Wars character
is a droid named K2-B4 a reference to both

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:56.140
Bryant's initials and his number 24 Los Angeles
Lakers jersey, which he began wearing during

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:01.100
the 2006-07 NBA season after changing from
his old number 8 jersey.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.300
K2-B4 appeared on three episodes of the third
season of the animated series Star Wars: The

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.180
Clone Wars, which takes place in the three
years between the Star Wars prequel movies

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.440
Attack of the Clones and Revenge of the Sith.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.970
A feminine character who uses "she/her" pronouns,
K2-B4 also sports a yellow and purple color

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.100
scheme the same palette that the Lakers use.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.600
According to StarWars.com, at the time the
Clone Wars animation department created K2-B4's

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.620
animation model, the Lakers had won the NBA
Championship.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:33.200
It's also been said that the inclusion of
K2-B4 on the series was a nod to crew members

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.240
who were Lakers fans.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.240
A T-series tactical droid, K2-B4 served the
Confederacy of Independent Systems.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.980
Her biography details that she experienced
a lot.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:46.420
She entered Lola Sayu, the planet containing
the Citadel, without being detected; she got

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:51.450
briefly tricked by battle droids transporting
R2-D2, resulting in her altering Citadel guards

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.890
to capture the droids; and she boarded a Lucrehulk
Droid Control Ship but unfortunately failed

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.800
to keep a group of Jedi masters out of Lola
Sayu.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.780
If only K2-B4's win rate was as good as Kobe
Bryant's!

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.940
As it happens, Kobe Bryant's ties to the Star
Wars property extend even further than K2-B4.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:12.000
To announce his then-impending retirement
from the NBA which came after the 2015-16

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.470
basketball season Bryant penned a poem entitled
"Dear Basketball," in which he discussed his

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.840
love for and dedication to the sport.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.920
Bryant eventually partnered with animator
and character designer Glen Keane, who has

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.350
credits in films like Aladdin, Beauty and
the Beast, and The Little Mermaid, to turn

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.600
"Dear Basketball" into an animated short film.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.520
"I'll always be that kid with the rolled up
socks, garbage can in the corner, five seconds

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.720
on the clock, ball in my hands."

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.500
With Keane animating and directing and Bryant
narrating from the poem he wrote, the biggest

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.100
thing left to do was find someone to score
the short.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.120
The person they settled on was none other
than John Williams, the legendary composer

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.280
who's scored the films in the Star Wars franchise.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.280
What's even more interesting is that "Dear
Basketball" went on to win the Oscar for Best

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:00.569
Animated Short Film at the 2018 Academy Awards
ceremony, and Star Wars actors Mark Hamill,

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:05.480
Oscar Isaac, and Kelly Marie Tran presented
Bryant and Keane with the shiny golden statue.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.740
Fans may even recall that Bryant walked onto
the basketball court to the infamous "Imperial

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:15.040
March" from Star Wars in December 2013 after
recovering from an Achilles tendon injury

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.350
that threatened to end his NBA career.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:22.350
He also once compared himself to the franchise's
most notorious villain, telling ESPN's Baxter

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:27.150
Holmes in December 2015 that he sees himself
as, quote, "Darth Vader, of course."

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:28.640
With a smile, Bryant added:

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.880
"But I don't take orders from the Emperor...so...Darth
Emperor, maybe."

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.840
"Darth Emperor" doesn't have quite the same
ring to it as his famous nickname "Black Mamba"

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:39.569
does, but no matter what you call him, Kobe
Bryant will always have a place in the Star

00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:40.480
Wars universe.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.000
"Heroes come and go."

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.680
"But legends are forever."

